<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Sergei Witte</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1849">1849</a>–<a href="/years/1915">1915</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Sergei Witte was a Russian statesman who served as minister of finance in the 1890s and early 1900s and later as prime minister. He promoted rapid industrialisation, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trans-siberian-railway">Trans-Siberian Railway</a> and the gold standard. His programme drove high industrial growth rates in the 1890s.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/sergei-witte.jpg" alt="Sergei Witte" width="320" height="415" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Chapiro, St. Petersburg · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:SergeiWitte01548v.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Sergei Yulyevich Witte was born on 29 June 1849. His father, from a Lutheran Baltic German family, converted to Russian Orthodoxy on marrying Yekaterina Fadeyeva, and Sergei was raised on the estate of his mother's parents. He studied at Novorossiysk University in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/odessa">Odessa</a>, graduating at the top of his class in 1870. He had planned an academic career in mathematics, but his relatives thought it unsuitable for a noble, and he was persuaded to enter the railways.</p><p>After six months of training on the Odessa Railways he became chief of the traffic office. A wreck in late 1875 cost many lives and led to a four-month prison sentence, but in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russo-turkish-war-1877-1878">Russo-Turkish War</a> he directed the Odessa Railways and attracted the notice of Grand Duke Nicholas Nikolaevich, who commuted the sentence to two weeks.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>From 1889 to 1891 Witte was Director of Railway Affairs in the Finance Ministry, expanding the railways under state control and assigning staff by merit rather than patronage. Appointed Minister of Finance in August 1892, he held the post for eleven years and nearly doubled the empire's revenues. He accelerated the construction of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trans-siberian-railway">Trans-Siberian Railway</a>, established a state alcohol monopoly in 1895 and, in 1896, placed the ruble on the gold standard, which increased investment and the inflow of foreign capital. In 1896 he concluded the Li-Lobanov Treaty with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/li-hongzhang">Li Hongzhang</a>, which gave <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a> the right to build the Chinese Eastern Railway.</p><p>Under pressure from the landed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gentry">gentry</a> and his enemies at court, he was moved in August 1903 to the chairmanship of the Committee of Ministers, a post with no real power. In 1905 the tsar sent him to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> to negotiate the end of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russo-japanese-war">Russo-Japanese War</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/portsmouth-new-hampshire">Portsmouth, New Hampshire</a>, where he is credited with negotiating well for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a>, and he was made a count.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">In office</h2><p>On 9 October 1905 Witte told <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nicholas-ii">Nicholas II</a> that the country faced a catastrophic revolution and offered two choices: a military dictator or broad reforms. He framed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/october-manifesto">October Manifesto</a>, and on 20 October 1905 he was appointed the first chairman of the Council of Ministers, effectively prime minister. With his Council he designed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a>'s first constitution, but within months court opposition discredited him as a reformer, and he resigned before the First Duma met on 10 May 1906. He remained a member of the State Council but never again held an administrative post. Witte died in St Petersburg in 1915. His memoirs, completed in 1912, were published in translation in 1921.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Sergei Witte” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote><b>Sergei Witte</b>'s industrialisation programme of the 1890s drove annual industrial growth of around 8 per cent, making Russia one of the world's five largest industrial economies by 1900.</blockquote><a href="/empire/russian-empire">Russian Empire · Socioeconomy</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tbilisi">Tbilisi</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/georgia">Georgia</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saint-petersburg">Saint Petersburg</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/russia">Russia</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=41.72,44.79&amp;year=1891" aria-label="Tbilisi on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5641 677 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Sergei Witte"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M6091 947h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M6091 947h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=41.72,44.79&amp;year=1891">What was here in 1891? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
